Current bug report: {'summary': 'createArchiveInputStream detects text files less than 100 bytes as tar archives', 'description': 'The fix for [](https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/COMPRESS-117 "Certain tar files not recognised by ArchiveStreamFactory") which modified ArchiveStreamFactory().createArchiveInputStream(inputstream) results in short text files (empirically seems to be those <= 100 bytes) being detected as tar archives which obviously is not desirable if one wants to know whether or not the files are archives.  '}

Recent bug reports (SBERT retrieval results with similarity): [{'similarity': 0.41, 'summary': 'Support writing entries > 8GiB in tar', 'description': 'We already parse PAX headers and the star/GNU tar/BSD tar dialects used for big  \nentries. Similar to the way we handle long file names there should be a user option to chose  \nbetween "error", "star" and "posix" or "pax". "star" is Jörg Schilling\'s tar which was the  \nfirst one to use the binary size representation later adopted by GNU and BSD tar as well.'}, {'similarity': 0.53, 'summary': 'Cannot Read Winzip Archives With Unicode Extra Fields', 'description': 'I have a zip file created with WinZip containing Unicode extra fields. Upon attempting to extract it with org.apache.commons.compress.archivers.zip.ZipFile, ZipFile.getInputStream() returns null for ZipArchiveEntries previously retrieved with ZipFile.getEntry() or even ZipFile.getEntries(). See UTF8ZipFilesTest.patch in the attachments for a test case exposing the bug.'}, {'similarity': 0.51, 'summary': 'Support POSIX/Pax variant for long file names in tar', 'description': 'Once we add support for writing Pax headers for [](https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/COMPRESS-165 "Support writing entries > 8GiB in tar") it will be pretty easy to support the same headers for long file names as well.'}]

